  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "I.Kondala Rao vs The State Of Andhra Pradesh on 11 September, 2019 Author: U. Durga Prasad Rao Bench: U. Durga Prasad Rao Hon'Ble Sri Justice U. Durga Prasad Rao Criminal Petition No.4967 Of 2019 Order:",
    "facts": "The petitioners/A1 to A5 seek to quash the proceedings against them in Crime No.277 of 2019 of Kancharapalem Police Station, Viakhapatnam City, which was registered against them for the offences under Sections 188, 272, 273 r/w 34 IPC, Section 7(1) r/w 20(1) of Cigarettes and Other Tobacco Products (Prohibition of Advertisement and Regulation of Trade and Commerce, Production, Supply and Distribution) Act, 2003 (for short, 'COTP Act, 2003') and Sections 48, 59 and 63 of Food Safety Standards Act (for short, 'FSS Act'). 2. The allegations against the petitioners briefly are that on 23.06.2019 at 17.30 hours, the complainant on receiving the credible information proceeded to Gnanapuram Railway Station Parcel Office, and seized two autos bearing Nos.AP 31TF 2645 and AP 31TF 4302 used for transporting banned tobacco products and also arrested the accused and on interrogation, they came to know that they were selling the Gutka Packets to pan shops at higher prices. Hence, the FIR is registered for the offences under Sections 188, 272, 273 r/w 34 IPC, Section 7(1) r/w 20(1) of COTP Act, 2003 and Sections 48, 59 and 63 of FSS Act. He relied upon the order of this Court in Crl.P.Nos.3731 and batch, dated 27.08.2018.",
    "arguments": "3. Learned counsel for petitioners while denying the FIR allegations would submit that even if the prosecution case is accepted to be true, the Gutka which is a tobacco product would not fall within the definition of Section 3(j) of Food Safety Standards Act (for short, 'FSS Act') and therefore, the police are not authorized to register the crime and investigate and file charge sheet and therefore, registration of the FIR is contrary to the provisions of FSS Act. 4. So far as the offence under Section 188 of IPC is concerned, as per Section 195(1)(a)(i) of Cr.P.C, no Court shall take cognizance of the said offence except on the complaint in writing of that Court or by such Officer of the Court as that Court may authorise in writing in this behalf, or of some other Court to which that Court is subordinate.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "3. Learned counsel for petitioners while denying the FIR allegations would submit that even if the prosecution case is accepted to be true, the Gutka which is a tobacco product would not fall within the definition of Section 3(j) of Food Safety Standards Act (for short, 'FSS Act') and therefore, the police are not authorized to register the crime and investigate and file charge sheet and therefore, registration of the FIR is contrary to the provisions of FSS Act.",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "4. So far as the offence under Section 188 of IPC is concerned, as per Section 195(1)(a)(i) of Cr.P.C, no Court shall take cognizance of the said offence except on the complaint in writing of that Court or by such Officer of the Court as that Court may authorise in writing in this behalf, or of some other Court to which that Court is subordinate."
  },
